A||||18||Richard Parnell||Mobile 1 or ATF?||||5||
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||12-14-1999||03:29 PM||||I know, I know, I'm a dummy but on the subject (again) of the hydraulic clutch.  Do I understand that some people are putting Mobile 1 oil in their clutch system. Or are the Mobile 1 and ATF the same?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Alex4mula||12-14-1999||04:00 PM||||They are different. Mobil 1 is engine oil. ATF is Autmatic Transmission Fluid. I used the AFT with two brand new master and slave. Don't know why but the dam thing shot both. Worked for 5 days and boom!. The master cylynder stoped releasing from the bore. The slave also had problems. Replaced everything and used Castrol Synthetic brake fluid. Smooth and nice. Others had good experiences but I will never use ATF again...||||reg||1
Z||000002||Richard Parnell||12-14-1999||04:03 PM||||Oh my god! Here we go again.  Every time I think I know which way to go, the information I think is correct, becomes suspect. Maybe I will go automatic, I'm losing it!||||reg||11
Z||000003||Patrick||12-14-1999||04:06 PM||||<BR>Hmmm.... sorry to hear that. This is the first report I've heard where there's been a problem. Hopefully it's just an aberration.<P>Richard, the answers to your questions are all in the original thread:<P> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/000034.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/000034.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Sage||12-14-1999||04:11 PM||||Richard; Auto are pretty nice, but they break too.  It does seem that I have heard quite a few problem tales about the fiero clutch cars tho.  I have 3 automatics and have had no trouble with any of them.  My son has an 88 coupe which I just finished putting a clutch in.  Will have to see how long it lasts.  I have had many standard trans cars and they are great at the race track but for town driving and general cruising, give me an automatic! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Alex4mula||12-14-1999||04:17 PM||||No more sorry than me....  I took the advice from here and I appreciate that. This is a good group and I read enough to convince me. The pedal feel was a little more stiff than I thought. If nobody have had any problems and they have more than 6 month with it then take my case as the .01% of times it does not work.... Don't know if blaming the ATF or two bad brand new pumps...||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||12-14-1999||04:37 PM||||what brand were the cylinders?||||reg||5
Z||000007||Eric||12-14-1999||05:51 PM||||I really didn't want to open my mouth before now, but...  I just got my 3.1 rebuild back on the road with a Centerforce Dual Friction clutch.  I was one of the people who were complaining about this system from the start and probably would've continued complaining for the last few months if I had not decided to rebuild my motor.  Anyway, I'm back on the road with a clutch which has a lighter feel (about 3 times lighter) than my old original unit.  At first I thought I lost hydralic pressure because the pedal was so light.  My theory is with the stock clutch, the pressure put on the master and slave cylinders blew out the seals and caused fluid to get out and air to get in.  I have the Getrag trans. and I can't speak for the pedal pressure of other transmissions.  I hope this new clutch does the trick and I don't mind being the guinea pig for now, so just keep your ears open and I tell you how the new setup is performing.<P>I know this thing is kind of expensive.  I got it for around $350 from Summit.  From what I've been told that's more than twice a stock replacement's cost.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Richard Parnell||12-14-1999||06:09 PM||||Eric- Did you do anything other than replace the clutch?  Or did you work on the flywheel also?||||reg||5
Z||000009||Richard Parnell||12-14-1999||06:11 PM||||Sorry I meant pressure plate. Not flywheel.||||reg||1
Z||000010||superXmotorsports||12-14-1999||07:45 PM||||If anyone else wants a Centerforce dual-friction I can sell them to you for $299.  I set up an account and then got scared off by FOCOA stories and bought the RAM powergrip Kevlar clutch.  SXM.||||reg||3
Z||000011||Eric||12-14-1999||10:28 PM||||I had the flywheel resurfaced, but I did nothing to the pressure plate other than bolt it on.  Richard, are you planning on modifying the motor or doing an engine swap?  If so I would recommend going to an aftermarket high performance clutch.  The Centerforce should be an excellent candidate except for a few bad reviews which can probably be explained by the popularity of the unit.  A certain amount are bound to fail when there are so many people using them.  It may do the trick in solving your clutch pedal problems.  Have you replaced the clutch line yet?  I had to buy a new one from the Fierostore because I believe the braided stainless lines were cracked on my old one. <P>Superx, have you tried out the Ram clutch yet?  If so tell us what you think of the pedal feel. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Cooter||12-14-1999||11:50 PM||||I am glad that I used jack oil in mine if the atf caused problems. . . But then again, I have not driven the car in a few days and when I get in it, there might not be any clutch left!||||reg||1
Z||000013||Alex4mula||12-15-1999||11:31 AM||||The cylinders brand is WAGNER. I had once a Centerforce clutch. Was the best I have in terms of clutch feeling (smooth) and griping power (no slipage). But failed a little bit earlier than expected in an ugly way. Whe I took it out the asbestos were almost in pieces. Anyway I would use it again... ||||reg||1
